  "interpretation": "<p>The&nbsp;&nbsp;result is negative for 22q12(EWSR1) gene rearrangement</p>\n<p><strong>Result table :</strong></p>\n<table border=\"1\" cellspacing=\"1\" cellpadding=\"1\" data-cke-table-faked-selection-table=\"\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"cke_table-faked-selection\"><strong>Chromosome Abnormality Screened</strong></td>\n<td class=\"cke_table-faked-selection\"><strong>Results</strong></td>\n<td class=\"cke_table-faked-selection\"><strong>Abnormal (%)</strong></td>\n<td class=\"cke_table-faked-selection\"><strong>Cutoff (%)</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"cke_table-faked-selection\">\n<p>EWSR1 (22q11.2) gene&nbsp;rearrangement</p>\n</td>\n<td class=\"cke_table-faked-selection\">\n<p>Negative</p>\n</td>\n<td class=\"cke_table-faked-selection\">\n<p>0</p>\n</td>\n<td class=\"cke_table-faked-selection\">\n<p>&ge;25% of nuclei</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>&nbsp; INSERT IMAGE HERE</p>\n<p><strong>Additional Information</strong></p>\n<p>Ewing sarcoma is the second most common malignant bone tumor in children and young adults. FISH analysis to identify a common translocation t(11;22)(q23;q12) or less&nbsp; &nbsp;frequently&nbsp; &nbsp;t(21;22)(q22;q12)&nbsp; &nbsp;or&nbsp; &nbsp;t(7;22)(p22;q12), plays an important role in the confirmation of clinical diagnosis. The translocation&nbsp;&nbsp; t (11;22)(q24;q12)&nbsp;&nbsp; has&nbsp;&nbsp; been detected in both fresh tumor cells derived by biopsy of primary &amp; metastatic tumors as well as in cell lines established from patients with Ewing sarcoma. It is found in 90-95% of patients with Ewing sarcoma. FISH can detect this rearrangement in interphase cells only (Li et al., 2019; Yan et al., 2018).</p>",
